/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Starting has its own history.
   - Cranking is rotating the engine before it fires.
   - N is engine speed in rpm; ω is angular speed in rad/s; 2π rad is one revolution and 60 s is one minute.
   - Cranking power P (W) is the rate of doing cranking work:
   - T is cranking torque in N·m; ω is angular speed in rad/s; P is cranking power in W.
   - Power needs both torque and speed.
   - Minimum starting speed is the lowest cranking speed for reliable first firing.
   - Engine resistance torque comes from compression, ring and bearing friction, valve-train friction, and pumping air through throttles and ports.
   - At 200 rpm the starter curve in the figure gives T = 65 − 0.1 × 200 = 45 N·m.
   - Compression ratio is the ratio of cylinder volume at bottom dead centre to volume at top dead centre.
*/
