/* book Worked example 2: Q: On a warm day the starter torque at the crankshaft is Ts = 65 − 0.1N and the engine resistance is Tr = 10 + 0.1N (N in rpm, torque in N·m). On a cold morning thick oil raises the resistance to Tr = 30 + 0.15N and the weaker battery lowers the starter line to Ts = 55 − 0.1N. (a) Find the stable cranking point and the cranking power on each day. (b) The engine needs at least 80 rpm to start. Will it start on the cold morning? What if it were a diesel needing 120 rpm?
   1. Warm: 65 − 0.1N = 10 + 0.1N, so 0.2N = 55 and N = 275 rpm; T = 65 − 0.1 × 275 = 37.5 N·m.
   2. Warm power: ω = 2π × 275 ÷ 60 = 28.80 rad/s; P = 37.5 × 28.80 = 1080 W.
   3. Cold: 55 − 0.1N = 30 + 0.15N, so 0.25N = 25 and N = 100 rpm; T = 55 − 0.1 × 100 = 45 N·m (check: 30 + 0.15 × 100 = 45 N·m).
   4. Cold power: ω = 2π × 100 ÷ 60 = 10.47 rad/s; P = 45 × 10.47 = 471 W.
   5. The cold torque is higher (45 against 37.5 N·m) but the speed falls from 275 to 100 rpm, so the power falls by more than half.
   6. 100 rpm is above the 80 rpm needed, so the petrol engine starts, with little margin; a diesel needing 120 rpm would not.
   Answer: Warm: 275 rpm, 37.5 N·m, 1080 W. Cold: 100 rpm, 45 N·m, 471 W. The petrol engine (80 rpm needed) starts on the cold morning; a diesel needing 120 rpm would not. */
