/* book exam traps: High torque at zero speed is not cranking power: P = Tω = 0 at stall. | Do not use engine rpm directly in P = Tω; convert to rad/s first. Using 200 instead of 20.94 gives a power about 9.55 times too large. | Diesel cranking torque is higher mainly because of high compression ratio, not because diesel fuel has higher calorific value during cranking. | Battery voltage sag under cranking current is not the same as battery capacity in Ah; a battery can have enough Ah and still fail cold cranking. | The stable cranking point is the intersection of starter torque and engine resistance curves, not the starter's maximum torque. */
