/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A starter is a DC motor built for one duty: maximum torque at zero speed, for a few seconds, a few times an hour.
   - In a series-wound motor the field winding is in series with the armature.
   - T is torque in N·m; k is a constant fixed by the winding design; φ is the flux per pole in webers, set by the field current; Ia is armature current in A; Eb is back emf in V; ω is angular speed in rad/s.
   - A permanent-magnet starter uses ferrite magnets instead of a field winding.
   - A gear-reduction starter spins a small, fast armature through a planetary or spur gear set, usually 3:1 to 5:1, so the pinion turns slowly with multiplied torque.
   - A coaxial starter puts the reduction gear on the armature axis, in line with the pinion.
   - Design variations follow the engine.
   - The drive pushes the pinion into mesh with the flywheel ring gear, passes cranking torque, and releases when the engine fires.
   - A Bendix inertia drive carries the pinion on a coarse helical screw thread on the armature shaft.
   - A pre-engaged drive uses the solenoid to swing a lever that pushes the pinion into mesh before the main contacts close.
*/
